            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book logically derives the symbol of the garden from the established symbol of the tree. Since trees represent nations, peoples, or kings (as explicitly decoded by Daniel in Dan 4 and Ezekiel in Ezek 31), a garden—a planted grouping of trees—represents a grouping of nations or peoples. The book provides strong scriptural evidence for this in Ezekiel 31:9 and 18, where the 'trees of Eden' in the 'garden of God' are explicitly identified as Pharaoh and his multitude among other nations. Furthermore, the book's interpretation elegantly resolves a textual difficulty in Isaiah 66:17, explaining 'in the gardens, after one' as 'among the nations, following one,' which flawlessly matches the surrounding context concerning the gathering of all nations without requiring the interpolation of the word 'tree' as traditional translations do. The counter-interpretation, while historically plausible for literal groves, requires adding words to Isaiah 66 and treats the garden in Ezekiel 31 merely as poetic color rather than a defined symbol. The book offers a superior, cohesive explanation of the provided texts.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A garden symbolically represents a grouping of nations or peoples.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"A planted grouping of trees\",\n    \"arranged together under a gardener's rule\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter applies this definition to the idolatrous gardens of Isaiah 1:29, 65:3, and 66:17, arguing that sacrificing 'in gardens' means adopting the practices of foreign nations. While textually elegant in Isaiah 66:17, this may overlook the historical reality of literal sacred groves in Canaanite and Israelite idolatry, though it does not strictly invalidate the symbolic layer.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"A garden literally refers to a cultivated plot of ground, often used in Scripture as a symbol for paradise, a sanctuary, or a place of flourishing. In prophetic rebukes (Isaiah 1, 65, 66), 'gardens' refer to literal sacred groves used for pagan worship. In Ezekiel 31, the 'garden of God' is an archetypal reference to Eden used metaphorically to describe the unparalleled glory of the world's greatest empires, not a strict equation where 'garden' universally equals a 'grouping of nations'.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"The garden is a place of cultivation, paradise, or divine presence, used contextually as a metaphor for flourishing or literal idolatrous groves, rather than uniformly symbolizing a grouping of nations.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter denies that 'garden' uniformly functions as a symbol for a grouping of nations, arguing instead that its metaphorical uses are contextual (e.g., Edenic archetypes) and its prophetic rebukes often literal (sacred groves).\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"In Isaiah 1:29 and 65:3, the Israelites literally sacrificed in sacred groves (gardens), a well-documented historical practice, making the literal reading stronger than the metaphorical 'among the nations'.\",\n    \"The equation of trees with nations in Ezekiel 31 does not mean 'garden' inherently means 'grouping of nations' everywhere; it is just the natural narrative setting for the tree metaphor.\",\n    \"Major biblical gardens (Eden, Gethsemane, New Jerusalem) are primarily characterized by divine presence, testing, or paradise, not by being 'groupings of nations'.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Provides a precise, scripturally derived definition built directly on the established symbol of the tree (Daniel 4, Ezekiel 31).\",\n    \"Accounts perfectly for Ezekiel 31:9, 18, where the 'garden of God' is literally populated by nation-trees.\",\n    \"Explains Isaiah 66:17 flawlessly in its immediate context (the gathering of nations in v.18-19) without needing to add the word 'tree' to the Hebrew text, as traditional translations are forced to do.\",\n    \"Maintains a consistent hermeneutic where the scale of the symbol (seed, tree, garden/forest) perfectly aligns with the scale of the referent (person, nation, group of nations).\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Accounts for the literal historical reality of sacred groves in ancient Near Eastern idolatry, which fits the rebukes in Isaiah 1 and 65.\",\n    \"Aligns smoothly with Genesis 2, where the garden of Eden is introduced before any 'nations' exist, focusing on God's provision and presence.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Ezekiel 31:9\",\n    \"Isaiah 66:17-19\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [\n    \"How the definition applies directly to the original Garden of Eden in Genesis 2, where multiple nations did not yet exist (though the book's framework of seed/person-to-nation growth might address this).\"\n  ],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
